From: =?UTF-8?q?John=20T=C3=B6rnblom?= <john.tornblom@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 10:59:29 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/6] Add a PS4 (Orbis) build

A build with the ps4-homebrew-dev SDK (ORBIS, __ORBIS__) makes the
programs of a PS4 package: stuff/cmake/modules/PS4.cmake. The PS4 build
script converts them and makes the package, which is laid out like any
Yamagi Quake II installation, in /app0: the client (eboot.bin), the
renderers next to it, and baseq2/ with the game and the game data.

The client is SDL_main(): SDL2main (from pkg-config's SDL2 libraries) has
main(), and sets the XDG base directories up in the title's download data
(/download0, 512 MiB), where the config and the savegames are, in
/download0/.data/YamagiQ2. Mesa's shader cache is there too, and is held
to 128 MiB. The game runs as whichever user the system starts it as.

The SDK's libraries are static. Only the client links SDL2, with
-rdynamic; the renderers and the game are shared objects, converted with
orbis-elf2self --main to use the client's SDL2 and C library. So the
renderers do not link SDL2, and may have undefined symbols. A shared
object converted that way gets the client's symbol for any that it exports
itself, and the PS4's compiler exports every symbol by default, whatever
-fvisibility says: the renderers and the game would share the client's
Com_Printf(), hunk and more. -fno-visibility-from-dllstorageclass makes
-fvisibility=hidden work as on other systems.

The client's folder is SDL_GetBasePath(): the kernel's path of the
executable need not be one that the game sees.

Graphics are Mesa's (zink on RADV): OpenGL 3.2 for the GL3 renderer and
OpenGL ES 3 for the GLES3 one, both through EGL.

The hunk is not asked to be aligned for superpages (MAP_ALIGNED_SUPER):
it is an optimization only, and the PS4's kernel is not known to take the
flag.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01EVpxQnEDpmH9ifdSUZDpqm
